{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head 2e12655cb5ce977b7d3a7441c17d06536220239b on task/first-ontology-round3, cut from current main 77a38f6. git merge-tree --write-tree main HEAD exits 0; go test ./internal/workroom ./internal/statusview both ok; make vet clean; make test clean across all packages; the UI suite 49 pass, 0 fail; git diff --check exits 0. The note is the only changed file.\n\nFINDING 1, the affordance denial. I read ui/src/components/Toolbar.tsx at the head I worked from before writing anything. semanticActions is lines 75-114 there, not 71-113 - your citation included the four-line comment above it and stopped one line early - and the file is byte-identical to the one at c4bfbb1, so every condition you named holds exactly: only the actor named in body.to is offered accept on an unclaimed request, only commitment.performer is offered mark done while promised, only commitment.requester is offered accept or needs work on a report, only statement.actor is offered withdraw, and a retired or ineffective statement offers nothing. Seven offers in all.\n\nOLD, anchoring section: 'This is the goal, not the state. No surface computes an affordance today; the presentation contract section says exactly how far each one has got.'\n\nNEW, anchoring section: 'This is the goal, not the state - but one surface has built a piece of it by hand, and the boundary runs through that piece rather than around it. semanticActions in ui/src/components/Toolbar.tsx computes a row's offered acts from the fold's state and the viewer's position: only the actor a request is addressed to is offered accept, only the performer of a promised commitment is offered mark done, only the requester of a reported one is offered accept or needs work, only a statement's own author is offered withdraw, and a retired or ineffective statement offers nothing at all. Those are the fold's own conditions, applied before the append rather than after it - the symmetry above, for the seven offers one row can make, in one component. What it is not is the projection this note proposes. The conditions are written in TypeScript and keyed off compiled-in kind names, not derived from the definitions in force; the vocabulary projection carries no affordances for it to read; it withholds an action without naming the condition that failed; and it covers one surface, so the CLI and the MCP server still offer nothing and refuse afterwards. Call it a hand-coded partial affordance projection.'\n\nOLD, Proposed/Affordances: '...This is the symmetry the anchoring use case turns on, and no part of it exists. Today a surface learns that a promise actor is wrong only after the fold refuses the appended act.'\n\nNEW, Proposed/Affordances: '...None of that is in the projection: the vocabulary carries definitions and binding state and no actor-relative judgement at all. What is built is the hand-coded stand-in named in the anchoring section, semanticActions in ui/src/components/Toolbar.tsx - the fold's conditions for seven row offers, written against compiled-in kind names, in one surface, offering no reason to the actor it withholds an action from. Its conditions and the fold's agree today because someone wrote them twice, which is the drift this contract exists to prevent. The CLI and the MCP server withhold nothing: they learn a promise actor is wrong when the fold refuses the appended act. Deriving the conditions from definitions, publishing them per actor, and giving every surface the same answer is unbuilt.'\n\nFINDING 2, the either/or placement. I read all four cited files at the head I worked from. util.ts belongsInRoom is still 77-81, matching your citation; work.ts moved, buildWorkProjection is 104-214 with the hard-coded request/propose topic root at line 118 and the commitment loop at 132, not 96-127; store.ts statementWeight is 243-268, and reads definition.lifecycle at 255 and definition.render at 263. Two things you could not have seen: statementWeight is still exported on current main - it has not been removed - but nothing calls it, and Stream.tsx's Card and CompactRow are likewise exported and unused, with every recorded statement rendering through one RecordedMessage row. So the card-weight path you cited is dead code today. I did not put it in the note: an assertion resting on an uncalled export would be true and useless, and would go wrong the moment someone deletes it. The live render readers are belongsInRoom at Stream.tsx:177, kindTint at Stream.tsx:788 and elsewhere, the dissent attachment at Stream.tsx:211, and the proposal tally at Stream.tsx:666.\n\nOLD, Built: 'The UI is the only surface reading it so far, and it reads it for real. A kind's render class comes from the definition rather than from a compiled-in list, and decides three things: how a badge is tinted, whether an act belongs in the room stream or the work drawer, and whether a statement tallies as a proposal. The lifecycle field is what finds requests. A vocabulary panel lists every definition with its guidance, satisfier, staleness and lifecycle.'\n\nNEW, Built: render 'controls three things in the UI: stream exclusion - belongsInRoom in ui/src/lib/util.ts keeps an act out of the room stream when its class is governance or artifact, and Stream.tsx skips those statements. That is an exclusion and not a placement: render says what the stream leaves out, and says nothing about what the Work drawer holds. tint - kindTint in the same file gives the dissent class the danger colour... Every other kind takes a neutral tint from a compiled-in map. the proposal tally - a statement whose class is proposal carries a ratification tally on its row.' Then: 'Lifecycle, not render, is what finds work. The fold keys the whole commitment loop off the declared lifecycle field - internal/workroom/fold.go reads request, promise and report lifecycles to decide what a promise may rest on, who may report, and what status a commitment holds - and publishes the result as the projection's commitments. buildWorkProjection in ui/src/lib/work.ts builds the Work drawer from those commitments, and roots each topic on the compiled-in kind names request and propose rather than on any declared field. So the two views are not an either/or, and an earlier draft of this note was wrong to say render decides between them. A request appears in the room stream, because its class is commitment rather than governance or artifact, and it is the root of a Work topic, because a commitment names it. Reading render as a router between two surfaces predicts the wrong thing.'\n\nTHIRD INSTANCE, found by sweeping every absence claim in the note. The CLI bullet said 'help text, required-field prompting and value completion come from nothing' and the MCP bullet described a wholly hand-written surface, and the Built paragraph opened 'The UI is the only surface reading it so far'. Since the write-time warning merged at 77a38f6, both other surfaces read the live vocabulary: warnUndefinedKind in cmd/gs/main.go and withKindWarning in cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go each call Vocabulary.UndefinedKindWarning, whose message names the kinds the room actually defines from the live definitions rather than a compiled-in set. All three sentences are now corrected, with the boundary stated: the read happens after the append, so it can warn but cannot offer. The standing-rule paragraph and amendment 1's 'route acts between surfaces' phrase carried the same two errors and are fixed with them.\n\nI checked every other absence claim in the note against main and found no fourth instance. Verified as still true: the four proposed definition kinds appear in no catalog; FieldConstraint carries exactly present, type, matches and one-of and the type set is exactly string, event-id, actor-ref and path-commit; BasisConstraint is {Kinds, Min, Max} and names no class; nothing checks an artifact commit's Rests-On trailer - gitstore.Graph parses those trailers only to draw the UI railway, and internal/workroom never touches a git commit; gs init writes only a kernel genesis descriptor and no ontology; foldInterpreted holds kind-def, fold-activation and roster and validateDefinition refuses to redefine them; the fold computes exactly the eight commitment statuses the note lists, disputed being a verdict and not a status; profiles do not exist; the MCP tool list is eight tools and state's body is an open object of strings.\n\nConditions not met: none. Nothing else in the note was touched.","body":{"artifact":"$art","head":"2e12655cb5ce977b7d3a7441c17d06536220239b","status":"ready-for-review"}}